# The applications this image ships, one Flathub id per line.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ONE ALLOW-LIST, read by two consumers with two very
# different stakes in it:
#
#   * marwanos-appctl treats it as a privilege boundary: an install or
#     uninstall request from the shell is refused unless the id it names is
#     in here, exactly. It used to carry this list inline, and moving it out
#     was only acceptable because the file lives in /usr/lib -- image state,
#     read-only at runtime under composefs -- so "root-owned list in a
#     root-only script" and "root-owned file the script reads" are the same
#     trust boundary. A list in /etc or /var would NOT be: those are writable
#     machine state, and the allow-list would stop being part of the image.
#
#   * marwanos-storeart treats it as a shopping list: every id here gets its
#     Flathub icon prefetched into /var/marwanos/store/icons so the store
#     screen renders from disk instead of from the network.
#
# The shell's catalogue (shell/src/catalogue.gd, AVAILABLE_APPS) mirrors these
# ids by hand -- trimmed to the same three in the same change, so the two
# agree today. A name there that is missing here produces a card whose press
# root refuses, which is the safe direction for the two to disagree in if
# they ever drift again. Phase 1 deletes all of it: marwand owns the app set
# and serves it over RPC.
#
# THE SET IS TWO, DOWN FROM EIGHT BY WAY OF THREE. Steam is the games library
# and Zen is a browser THE RAIL NO LONGER SHOWS (2026-08-11: "zen is no longer
# the desired browser"): it keeps installing because the stores screen's buy
# flow opens Valve's checkout in it, and a purchase route with no browser is a
# dead end -- but the shell hides its card (catalogue.gd, RAIL_HIDDEN_APPS)
# and offers no available-card for it. Deleting Zen from THIS file too is the
# Chromium kiosk-shell session's move, made when the checkout has somewhere
# else to open. One app per job, no job twice, and browsing files is the
# shell's own Files screen rather than an app at all.
# Kodi left on 2026-08-11 on the owner's word -- "kodi is out no need to have
# it at all" -- taking the media-player job with it: a video opened from the
# Files screen now says it has no player, which is honest until the use
# returns. The five before it (Dolphin, RetroArch, VLC, Moonlight, Spotify)
# each duplicated a job something here already does; any id can come back
# with one line here and one in the display_name table when the use arrives.
#
# Lines starting with # and blank lines are ignored. Ids only -- no globs, no
# comments on the same line as an id. Matching everywhere is exact string
# equality, so a stray space here is an app that silently stops existing.
com.valvesoftware.Steam
app.zen_browser.zen
